.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt MSEAL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mseal
.Nd seal a range of mappings against change
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_mseal "void *addr" "size_t len" "unsigned long flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn mseal
seals the mappings of the range of
.Fa len
bytes at
.Fa addr ,
as Linux's call does.
.Fa addr
must be page-aligned;
.Fa len
is rounded up to a page, and an empty range seals nothing.
Every page of the range must be mapped.
A mapping the range cuts is split at its ends, and each piece inside
is sealed; sealing a sealed mapping again is no error, and no call
takes a seal away.
.Pp
A sealed mapping keeps its place, size and protection for as long as
the address space lasts.
These are refused with
.Er EPERM :
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Xr munmap 2
of a range that meets it, which unmaps nothing;
.It
.Xr mremap 2
of it, and
.Dv MREMAP_FIXED
onto it;
.It
.Xr mmap 2
with
.Dv MAP_FIXED
over it;
.It
.Xr mprotect 2
and
.Xr pkey_mprotect 2
of it, which change the mappings below it in the range first;
.It
.Xr madvise 2
advice that would discard its pages,
.Dv MADV_DONTNEED ,
.Dv MADV_DONTNEED_LOCKED ,
.Dv MADV_FREE ,
.Dv MADV_DONTFORK
and
.Dv MADV_WIPEONFORK ,
when it is anonymous private memory without write, which advises the
mappings below it first;
.It
a
.Xr brk 2
that would shrink the heap through it, which leaves the break as it was.
.El
.Pp
.Xr shmdt 2
of a sealed System V attachment leaves it and returns 0, as Linux does.
The seal does not stop
.Xr mlock 2 ,
the stack's growth, or the end of the mapping at
.Xr execve 2
and exit; a child that
.Xr fork 2
makes has the mapping sealed too.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /smaps
gives a sealed mapping the flag
.Ql sl .
.Pp
.Fa flags
must be 0.
Linux seals on 64-bit kernels alone: on the i686 kernel every call
fails with
.Er EPERM ,
and the x86-64 kernel seals for an i386 program as for a 64-bit one.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0 on success.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOMEM]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
is not 0,
.Fa addr
is not page-aligned, or the range wraps.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
A page of the range is not mapped, and nothing is sealed; or a split
cannot obtain its entry.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The kernel is the i686 one.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr brk 2 ,
.Xr pkey_alloc 2
.Sh STANDARDS
Linux 6.10 added the call.
